  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nToday - Tuesday: Dry and Well Above Normal Temperatures \n\nGenerally benign conditions expected for the start of the work week \nas weak ridging aloft moves overhead. This, combined with weak \nsurface high pressure moving into the Central Plains and Mid \nMississippi Valley, should keep conditions dry through Tuesday.\nDespite the passage of this mornings \"cold\" front, continued \nnorthwest flow, and low sun angle, clear skies and steady \ndiurnal radiation have allowed for temperatures to climb into \nthe mid 50s to low 60s. A warm air advection regime will set up \ntomorrow as winds back to the southwest ahead of yet another dry\nfrontal boundary Monday night into Tuesday. 08.13 NBM \nprobabilities for high temperatures at or above 60 degrees on \nMonday are in the 50-80% range for most locations south of the \nChimney - the highest probabilities across NE Iowa and in the \nriver valleys of Wisconsin. This WAA continues to a lesser \ndegree into Tuesday with highs still in the mid to upper 50s for\nmost locations. The low 60s will likely be limited to Southwest\nWisconsin as cloud cover starts to move in from the west during\nthe afternoon hours, limiting peak heating. \n\nWednesday - Thursday: Rain Chances Return, Significant Cool Down \n\nThe weak ridging from before breaks down as an upper wave moves \nsouth and east through the upper level troughing regime over the \nNortheastern CONUS by mid week. As this wave and its corresponding \nmid-level vorticity lobe interact with increasing mid-level moisture \nacross the Upper Midwest, this should be enough to create some \nlight precipitation chances across the area during the day on \nWednesday. LREF 08.00 and NBM 08.13 guidance has come into \nbetter consensus, allowing for an increase in PoPs from earlier \nforecasts. The best overlap of lift and moisture remains south \nand east of our area where rain chances and QPF are higher. LREF\n08.00 grand ensemble (GEFS, EPS, GEPS) probabilities for 0.10 \ninches of precipitation or more remain very low (less than 20%) \nacross the Driftless Region through 00Z Thursday. NBM 75th \npercentile rainfall values barely eclipse 0.05 inches across the\narea so it is highly likely that precipitation across the area \nwill be very light - owing in part to a noticeable layer of low \nlevel dry air in forecast soundings. Precipitation is expected \nto stay in the form of rain with a very, very low chance of some\nsnow mixing in if enough cold air can push south on the \nbackside of the system while precipitation is falling. This \ncooler air will eventually work its way into the area, dropping \ntemperatures quite a bit for the back half of the work week with\nhighs Thursday into Friday struggling to climb into the low \n40s. \n\nNext Weekend: High Pressure Sets In \n\nA Canadian high pressure is expected to sink south into the Upper \nMidwest by Friday, dominating much of the weather pattern across the \nCentral CONUS by Saturday. This will keep conditions dry so apart \nfrom the light rain chances on Wednesday, a dry forecast is on tap \nfor the area through at least next Monday. Temperatures will be at \ntheir coolest Thursday into Friday with some recovery expected by \nSaturday into the 50s.",
